/-!
# Relative components of the chosen natural flag

The literal symplectic common-Q join is now faithful maximal. In its Sp₄(4)
and G₂(4) quotient branches, the preceding normalized 7.0.1 and 3.2.5
conclusions recognize its original chosen module naturally, without assuming
a triangle orientation or using nonbinary elimination. The SL₃(4) row,
common-normal existence, reselected point placement and both orientations
remain with this owner; the earlier source conclusions remain explicit.

The field-four triangle now has its actual other residual K₀: it is the
unique member of L(G,T) inside K distinct from K and L₁, is T-invariant
and belongs to He, has literal PSL₂(4) two-core quotient, and generates
K together with L₁. Both residuals use the same Sylow marking and quotient
map. The Cartan intersection is now proved: X ∩ K has order three and
equals both X ∩ L₁ and X ∩ K₀. Once the source common normal subgroup is
supplied, the two residuals lie in one relatively normal component. That
component is their literal join I, belongs to L(G,T) and He, and equals
both the solvable residual and O²(IT). A nontrivial retained fixed layer
supplies the common normal subgroup directly. The faithful-layer
common-normal argument, recognition of I and its two orientations remain.
In the symplectic branch the two literal determinant characters now prove
X = (X ∩ L₁)(X ∩ L₂). Hence X lies in I, and the common normal subgroup
gives three-rank two for I and its actual two-core quotient. This is also
applied directly in the nontrivial fixed-layer component construction.
The strict first-component hypothesis now forces C_V(L) = 1 by nonsolvable
uniqueness. In the symplectic branch, a nonzero central seed and generation
by the two residuals prove that L₂ moves the Sylow omega-center. This
supplies faithful-family membership for the actual common-normal join I,
without assuming a recognized quotient or a new natural action. The
common-normal existence, model recognition and maximal-local clauses remain.
The symplectic third-vertex sieve now proves that both marked residuals
are proper in I, and excludes a PSL₃(4) quotient using the actual Cartan
and Sylow intersections. Its three-rank also excludes prime-field PSL₂,
PSL₂(25) and J₁. The common-normal consumer applies the full existing
embedding catalogue at I; the remaining triality and field-four rows
still require the marked GL₂(4) comparison before three-model recognition.

The common-Q symplectic third vertex now has an actual homogeneous chosen
module in R₂(IT), constructed without assuming maximality. Its normalized
faithful maximal overcomponent inherits Hypothesis 12.2.3 and a chosen module.
The literal L₂ embedding supplies the complete catalogue there; cubic-rank
and order comparisons exclude every binary Lie containing row in a strict
enlargement. Only exceptional field-four overcomponents remain in that
maximality comparison. Natural action on the reselected module, common-normal
existence, source SL₃(4) exclusion and both orientations remain open.

The contained components and their relative normality are proved from the
preceding flag's actual rank-one quotient models. The complete source alternatives
are specified with literal quotient models, including the field-four triangle.
The original conditional uniqueness deduction uses an Irr⁺ action on V₂.
For central covers at field four, the new deduction instead uses Irr⁺ on
[V₂,L₂]. The preceding central-rank bound controls its quotient, and perfectness
kills the other components' actions on the entire original plane. The literal
natural-cover and second-normalizer refinement consumers retain C_V(L).

The full-radical argument proves component uniqueness at every field degree:
the other components' Sylow intersections lie in R₂, and R₂-centralization
forces their action images to be trivial. Its source consumer retains that
preceding radical-action premise. The two odd-extension rows give the exact
two-odd quotients required by the first-normalizer refinement. The containing
first component also lies in the literal action kernel, yielding its trivial
intersection with a Cartan factor faithful on V₁.
The faithful cubic factor excludes both alternating-seven rows, six centerless
sporadic rows and the actual triple Mathieu cover. The last exclusion uses
a kernel-checked exponent-three Sylow subgroup in the existing binary model.
The literal Cartan consumer reduces the field-four list to the two odd-extension
refinements or four explicit remaining model classes. The prime-field PSL₂
row now gives the complete refinement, including its original SL₃(4) exclusion,
by the actual O²(X₀T) normalizer argument. Its Cartan consumer leaves the
three square-field, signed ternary and J₁ classes for further analysis.
The symplectic, G₂, Ree and both genuine triality Lie rows are excluded for
every nonbinary field. Primitive split primes and the concrete seven-plane
handle the odd-degree cases. For Ree, perfectness and quasisimplicity come
from the actual C-component; its core-free maximal parabolic kills the center,
so the outer-automorphism theorem requires no added simplicity premise.
The linear Lie rows force field four, retaining both SL₃(4) and PSL₃(4) until
the exceptional triangle argument. The final literal Cartan application
handles the complete embedding disjunction in every degree: outside field
four the first containing component equals L₁; only the stated field-four
models remain in addition to the established refinement alternatives.

The PSL₂(25) branch now supplies an actual element of T intersect K
acting outwardly on the literal L₁/O₂(L₁). Its lift uses the ambient
two-core map and the full Sylow intersection. Under the preceding
pointwise L₁ action, it centralizes the entire original V₁. The natural-model
normalizer calculation identifies its operator with a point-fixing model
element. Lifting that element to the literal first solvable residual makes
the same action inner and excludes PSL₂(25). The signed ternary field-five
rows give the same contradiction: their actual involution-centralizer
covers project to PGL₂(5), whose full Sylow cannot act innerly on A₅.
For J₁, the actual Sylow-normalized A₅ section and the original Sylow
normalizer generate the normal two-supplement. The preceding bound on
N_G(T) puts K in N_G(L), where component uniqueness forces K = L₁.
The updated Cartan sieve leaves only SL₃(4) and PSL₃(4) as exceptional
models, alongside the established refinement alternatives. In the PSL₃(4)
row, the whole original Cartan gives a faithful inner-diagonal action after
passage through the ambient two-core. Its image is PGL₃(4), identifying the
literal (K ⊔ X)/O₂(K ⊔ X) quotient. For the SL₃(4) covering row, a fixed
Cartan cubic lifts to a noncentral order-three element in the first component's
PSL₂(4) quotient. Together with the center of the SL₃(4) component quotient,
it gives an elementary abelian group of order nine fixed by an independent
outside cubic, contradicting the inherited three-rank bound. Thus the checked
first-index reduction has only the genuine refinement alternatives or the
literal PGL₃(4) quotient; it does not assert the remaining triangle clauses.

The rank-one embedding branches now give actual equality for binary PSL₂
quotients, or force field four and the source exceptional-prime or PSL₂(25)
row for odd prime and prime-square quotients. Their applications retain both
flag normalizers and arbitrary nontrivial U ≤ V₁ with the literal N_T(U)
Sylow condition. The first-normalizer consumer proves the full refinement
in these branches without assuming the exhaustive embedding list.

The split nonbinary SL₃, PSL₃, Sp₄ and G₂ carrier branches now match the
original binary field through their actual parabolic residual quotients.
The Ree branch selects its linear residual and exact coefficient field,
excluding the Suzuki residual. The applications include both flag
normalizers and arbitrary nontrivial U ≤ V₁. Their first-normalizer
consumer gives equality or the actual field-four PSL₃/PGL₃ pair without
assuming the exhaustive embedding list.

The genuine triality carriers now give both exact field relations, including
the cubic-subfield endpoint ³D₄(2). Their relative embedding applications
retain both original flag normalizers and arbitrary nontrivial U ≤ V₁.
The first-normalizer consumer combines these relations with the Cartan rank
exclusions to give equality without an embedding-list premise.
The complete rank-two Theorem C catalogue is now covered: solvability of
proper full-Sylow overgroups gives equality for PSL₃(2), PSp₄(2)′, G₂(2)′
and the Tits carrier. The other rows give the exact Lie alternatives.
Its first-normalizer consumer leaves equality or the literal field-four
PSL₃/PGL₃ pair, without an exhaustive embedding-list premise.

The Bender, even SU₃, A₅, A₆ and central 3.A₆ quotient carriers now force
equality at both original flag normalizers, by lifting solvability of
proper full-Sylow overgroups through the literal two-core. Their second
index feeds both the full-radical and field-four central-kernel uniqueness
arguments on the entire V₂. For A₇, 3.A₇ and J₁, the actual smaller section
forces field four without assuming its two-core vanishes. These embeddings
apply to both flag normalizers and arbitrary nontrivial U ≤ V₁. The
first-normalizer consumer gives equality using the faithful Cartan cubic
and J₁ generation, without an exhaustive embedding-list premise.

The M₂₂, central 3.M₂₂, M₂₃ and HS carriers now force field four in both
normalizer settings while preserving the smaller two-core quotient. Their
first-normalizer refinements use the original faithful Cartan factor. At
the second normalizer the actual natural-plane map bounds full V₂, including
C_V(L), by six binary dimensions. The containing perfect component is
generated by its 11-elements, so its action on this small space is trivial,
contradicting the original natural L₂ action. This excludes all four rows
at the second index without a faithfulness premise on K or an irreducibility
premise on full V₂.

The Hall--Janko carrier now forces field four by its literal full-Sylow
overgroup orders, with both flag-normalizer and arbitrary-subspace
applications. The first refinement uses the faithful Cartan cubic. At the
second normalizer, a nontrivial action would force a factor twenty-five in
the order of GL(6,2); the action-kernel argument rules this out without
assuming faithfulness on K. For J₁, J₄ and Ru, the checked carrier orders
and prime-generation argument likewise exclude the second-normalizer
rows on the whole original plane. J₄ and Ru only need the upper bounds
on field degree from section-order divisibility for this action argument;
their exact field-four embedding statements are supplied below.

The signed odd prime ternary rows now force both the original field-four
parameter and the containing prime five, retaining the actual smaller
two-core and both special covers. A central involution gives the genuine
SL₂(5) smaller image in the projective carrier. These embeddings apply at
both flag normalizers and arbitrary eligible U ≤ V₁. At the first normalizer
they feed the checked outer-Sylow contradiction. At the second, the containing
component's central quotient has order divisible by 125, incompatible with
a nontrivial action on the full plane of binary dimension at most six.
Both refinements use the original maps, without an exhaustive embedding-list
or field-four premise.

The two nonsplit odd-extension carriers now force field four through the
solvable-kernel projection to odd PSL₂ and its two-rank bound, retaining
the smaller two-core and both source normalizer settings. Their first
refinements identify the literal two-odd quotient without an assumed field.
At the second normalizer all rank-one carrier rows give equality, and
the elementary p-kernel extension is excluded by prime generation and the
full-plane GL(6,2) order. The rank-one equality also feeds the central-kernel
and full-radical uniqueness arguments on all of V₂, including C_V(L).

The Rudvalis carrier now also forces field four in both source normalizer
settings, retaining the smaller two-core. A full-Sylow automorphism bound
handles a trivial smaller core; the actual two-local order obstruction
handles a nontrivial core. The first-normalizer Cartan exclusion therefore
has no extra field-matching premise. Together with the existing action
exclusion on full V₂, both Rudvalis refinement rows are discharged.

The J₄ carrier now also forces field four in both source normalizer
settings and the arbitrary-subspace clause. The actual exponent-three
Sylows exclude field eight. A full-Sylow automorphism bound and the
certified normal elementary-subgroup catalogue exclude field thirty-two,
without discarding the smaller two-core. The first-normalizer faithful
Cartan cubic then excludes J₄ without an extra field-matching premise;
the existing second-normalizer exclusion retains the entire V₂.

The higher binary carriers A₈, PSL₄(2), and PSL₅(2) are excluded
with the smaller two-core retained. The M₁₁/M₁₂ local-order arguments,
M₂₄ exponent-three and local A₅ exclusions, and Held maximal-normalizer
catalogue now discharge every excluded simple carrier. The actual local
SQTK hypothesis supplies the full Theorem C catalogue, so its simple-quotient
embedding conclusion has no remaining carrier premise.

The actual A.3.6 source also supplies the complete central-three-cover
catalogue. Its binary linear, binary unitary, signed odd ternary, alternating
and Mathieu rows all give the original same-field alternative. Thus 11.1.1's
model list is proved whenever K/O₂(K) is quasisimple, both at the original
flag normalizers and at every eligible nontrivial subspace of V₁. All original
quotients, the smaller two-core and the full V₂ are retained.

The nonquasisimple source branch is now derived from A.3.6, including both
exceptional-prime congruences for the elementary extension. Combined with
the quasisimple catalogue, it proves the complete 11.1.1 alternative at
both flag normalizers and every eligible subspace of V₁. The existential
applications include containment, relative normality and uniqueness.
The first-index sieve consequently has no embedding-list premise; its
literal PGL₃(4) branch still requires the field-four triangle construction.

At the second normalizer the literal cubic Cartan factor now excludes
both A₇ and its central three-cover. Coprime lifting preserves the smaller
two-core and supplies a commuting five-element. The ordinary quotient has
no element of order fifteen; in the triple cover the cubic is central.
Chief-factor detection and the faithful binary degree-twelve bound make
that cubic fix all of V₂, contradicting the preceding faithful Cartan
plane action. The updated existential catalogue retains relative normality
and He membership, leaving only equality, SL₃/PSL₃ and the odd-radical row.

The linear-three rows at the second normalizer are excluded using the
literal smaller two-core and the action on the full original plane. The
marked SL₃ and Sp₄ applications discharge their action premises directly.
The remaining field-four odd-radical row supplies a canonical signalizer
fixing full V₂. Its genuine 1.3.4 embedding lies in the orbit of a component
distinct from the first containing component. Commutation modulo the local
two-core and the signalizer residual identity give normalization by L₁.
Nonsolvable uniqueness then forces the second containing component to be L₂.
Thus the second-index component equality, relative normality, He membership
and unique noncentrality are proved at every field degree, retaining the
preceding flag, Cartan, natural-action and full-radical inputs.

Both field-four triangle orientations at the first normalizer remain
obligations before the full source-family conclusion. Final assembly must
also discharge the displayed preceding inputs.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith, combined manuscript, 1 March 2004,
